 the " [:a {:href "/results/1901"} "18 km race"] ", although they had never won an international relay before. The three medal winners were on the team, while " [:a {:href "/athletes/98460"} "Nils Täpp"] " replaced " [:a {:href "/athletes/87794"} "Nils Karlsson"] ", who concentrated on the " [:a {:href "/results/1902"} "50 km"] ". Täpp had won a special relay selection race in St. Moritz."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The relay had little suspense. " [:a {:href "/athletes/99312"} "Nils Östensson"] " gave Sweden a commanding lead on the first leg, almost two minutes ahead of Finland’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/97542"} "Lauri Silvennoinen"] ". On Norway’s opening leg, " [:a {:href "/athletes/85583"} "Erling Evensen"] " had an accident with one of his skis and was only fourth at the first exchange, 13 seconds behind Austria, but " [:a {:href "/athletes/99307"} "Olav Økern"] " was over two minutes faster than Austria’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/85180"} "Josef Deutschmann"] " on the second leg and brought Norway into third place."] "\n\n" [:p {} "After the second exchange, the 11 competing teams held their positions. On the final leg, " [:a {:href "/athletes/91969"} "Martin Lundström"] " easily brought Sweden another gold medal, almost nine minutes ahead of Finland, with Norway another 3:27 behind. Sweden’s winning margin is by far the greatest in the history of the Olympic cross-country skiing relay."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitors"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Time"] [:th {} "Exchange"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "8"] [:td {} "Sweden"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/SWE"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/SWE.png"}] "SWE"]] [:td {} "2-32:08"] [:td {} "– (–)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [